>
  <p>
    Have you opened a website or navigation tool only to find your location displayed as a completely different city, state, or even another country? This is one of the most widespread issues encountered on the modern web.
  </p>
  <p>
    In this guide, we break down the four primary root causes behind incorrect location detection and provide step-by-step solutions to fix them.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Root Cause #1: You Are on Desktop (IP-Based Geolocation Fallback)</h2>
  <p>
    Unlike smartphones and tablets, most desktop PCs and laptops do not contain a dedicated hardware GNSS/GPS chip. When a desktop browser attempts to determine your location, it falls back to <strong>IP address geolocation</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>How IP Geolocation Works:</strong> Your Internet Service Provider (ISP) assigns your modem a public IP address from a large regional pool. Geolocation databases (such as MaxMind or IP2Location) map this IP range to your ISP's regional routing hub or corporate headquarters, not your physical front door.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The Symptom:</strong> If you live in a suburb or rural town, your IP address might register to a major city 50 to 100 kilometers away where your ISP's gateway router resides.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The Fix:</strong> Check your current network coordinates on our <a href="/my-location/ip/">My IP Location Page</a>. For true street-level accuracy on a desktop, connect to Wi-Fi rather than an Ethernet cable so your browser can detect nearby router BSSIDs.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Root Cause #2: Active VPNs or Proxy Extensions</h2>
  <p>
    If you use a Virtual Private Network (VPN), Tor browser, or corporate proxy, your internet traffic is encrypted and routed through an intermediary server:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    When a VPN tunnel is active, your browser reveals the IP address and physical coordinates of the VPN egress server. For example, if you are located in Dallas but connected to a Chicago VPN node, websites will identify your location as Chicago.
  </blockquote>
  <h3>How to Fix:</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Temporarily disable your VPN software or browser extension.</li>
    <li>Refresh the <a href="/my-location/">Where Am I</a> page.</li>
    <li>Verify that your detected city and coordinates now reflect your true local position.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>3. Root Cause #3: Relocated Wi-Fi Router (Stale BSSID Cache)</h2>
  <p>
    Did you recently move homes and bring your Wi-Fi router with you? 
    Both Google and Apple maintain massive crowd-sourced databases that associate every Wi-Fi router's unique hardware address (BSSID) with its physical latitude and longitude coordinates.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you move to a new apartment or city, your router retains its old physical coordinates in these databases for several weeks. Any smartphone or computer connected to your Wi-Fi will be incorrectly localized to your old address!
  </p>
  <h3>How to Fix a Stale Wi-Fi Location:</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Walk Outside with Mobile GPS:</strong> Take your smartphone outside your home, connect to your Wi-Fi, open Google Maps or Apple Maps with high-accuracy GPS enabled, and wait for a blue dot lock. The phone will broadcast an updated BSSID-to-GPS pairing to Apple and Google's mapping servers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Append "_nomap" to Your Wi-Fi SSID:</strong> If you want to opt out of Google's location database entirely, rename your Wi-Fi network with <code>_nomap</code> at the end (e.g., <code>HomeNetwork_nomap</code>).
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>4. Root Cause #4: Browser Location Permission Revoked or Cached</h2>
  <p>
    If you previously clicked "Block" when your browser prompted for location permissions, the website cannot access your device's GPS chip and is forced to display an inaccurate IP guess.
  </p>
  <h3>How to Reset Permissions:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Google Chrome (Desktop):</strong> Click the "Tune" icon or padlock next to the URL in the address bar &gt; find <strong>Location</strong> &gt; switch to <strong>Allow</strong> &gt; reload the page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Safari (macOS):</strong> Go to <strong>Safari &gt; Settings &gt; Websites &gt; Location</strong> &gt; find <code>mylocationlab.com</code> &gt; select <strong>Allow</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Safari (iOS / iPhone):</strong> Go to <strong>Settings &gt; Safari &gt; Location &gt; Ask or Allow</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Chrome (Android):</strong> Tap the three dots menu &gt; <strong>Settings &gt; Site settings &gt; Location</strong> &gt; ensure location access is enabled.
    </li>
  </ul>

